          "role": "assistant",
          "content": "The laundromat on Alder Street had a shelf marked LOST PROPERTY. Most of the time it held one mitten, a plastic dinosaur, and the paperback nobody would admit to reading. Then, one Thursday, someone left a photograph of a woman standing beside a refrigerator in the middle of a field.\n\nShe wore slippers. An extension cord ran from the refrigerator toward the edge of the picture. On the back, in blue ink, somebody had written, \u201cIt worked.\u201d By Saturday, three people had turned the photograph over to see if the other side offered a more satisfying explanation than that one.\n\nThe owner of the laundromat, Mrs. Patel, set a jar beside the photograph and invited customers to write down their theories. A boy proposed that the woman was trying to cool the whole world. His mother suggested that somebody had promised to help her move and had simply not arrived.\n\nA week later, the woman herself came in with a bag of towels. She recognized the photograph and laughed. \u201cThat\u2019s my sister\u2019s field,\u201d she said. \u201cWe had a picnic, and she didn\u2019t want anybody putting warm potato salad back in the cooler.\u201d She read every slip in the little jar.\n\nBefore leaving, she wrote a note of her own and folded it twice. Mrs. Patel waited until the towels were gone to open it. \u201cTell the boy we tried his idea first,\u201d it said. \u201cWe left the door open all afternoon, but it only got colder if you stood close.\u201d",
